s/  Linear maps satisfy `g\(t w\) = t g\(w\)`\./  Linear maps satisfy `g(t w) = t g(w)`. Inverses: substituting `k^-1(w)` for `w`\n  and `σ^-a(t)` for `t` in `k(t w) = σ^a(t) k(w)` gives `k^-1(t w) = σ^-a(t) k^-1(w)`./;
s/- \*\*Bieri--Strebel\.\*\* A finitely presented group/- **Bieri--Strebel.** This is the only step that uses it: it turns finite\n  presentation of `G` into one-sided finite generation of `M`. A finitely presented group/;
s/  The source is R\. Bieri and R\. Strebel, \*Almost finitely presented soluble\n  groups\*, Comment\. Math\. Helv\. 53 \(1978\); the theorem number is not pinned\./  The source is R. Bieri and R. Strebel, *Almost finitely presented soluble\n  groups*, Comment. Math. Helv. 53 (1978) 258--278; the theorem number is not\n  pinned. The statement is checked against the secondary source D. Cooper and\n  S. Tillmann, arXiv:1506.01793v1, §3.1, which also proves it in §3.3./;
s/- `Z ≀ Z` is not finitely presented \(G\. Baumslag, Math\. Z\. 75, 1961\), and/- `Z ≀ Z` is not finitely presented (G. Baumslag, Math. Z. 75 (1961) 22--28; also\n  item 4 applied to `Z[t,t^-1] ⋊ Z`, whose base is not finitely generated over\n  `Z[t]` or over `Z[t^-1]`), and/;
